        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.782,
            0.104,
            0.899,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "MATHEMATICS"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.139,
            0.252,
            0.158
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "7.4 Summary"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.165,
            0.579,
            0.184
          ],
          "content": "In this chapter, you have studied the following points :"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.193,
            0.835,
            0.216
          ],
          "content": "**1.** The distance between $\\mathrm { P } ( \\boldsymbol { x } _ { 1 } , \\boldsymbol { y } _ { 1 } )$ and $\\mathrm { Q } ( x _ { 2 } ,   y _ { 2 } )$ is $\\sqrt{ \\left( x_{2} - x_{1} \\right)^{2} + \\left( y_{2} - y_{1} \\right)^{2} }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.12,
            0.222,
            0.709,
            0.245
          ],
          "content": "**2.** The distance of a point $\\mathrm { P } ( x , y )$ from the origin is $\\sqrt { x ^ { 2 } + y ^ { 2 } }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.12,
            0.246,
            0.901,
            0.291
          ],
          "content": "**3.** The coordinates of the point $\\mathrm { P } ( x , y )$ which divides the line segment joining the points $\\mathrm { A } ( \\boldsymbol { x } _ { \\mathrm { 1 } } , \\boldsymbol { y } _ { \\mathrm { 1 } } )$ and $\\mathbf { B } ( x _ { _ 2 } , y _ { _ 2 } )$ internally in the ratio $m _ { \\mathrm { ~ l ~ } } : m _ { \\mathrm { ~ 2 ~ } }$ are"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.293,
            0.426,
            0.336
          ],
          "content": "\\left( \\frac{m_{1}x_{2} + m_{2}x_{1}}{m_{1} + m_{2}}, \\frac{m_{1}y_{2} + m_{2}y_{1}}{m_{1} + m_{2}} \\right).",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.338,
            0.898,
            0.404
          ],
          "content": "**4.** The mid-point of the line segment joining the points $\\mathrm { P } ( \\boldsymbol { x } _ { 1 } , \\boldsymbol { y } _ { 1 } )$ and $\\mathbb{Q}(x_{2},y_{2})$ is $\\left( \\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2}, \\frac{y_{1} + y_{2}}{2} \\right)$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.372,
            0.432,
            0.623,
            0.453
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "A NOTETOTHE READER"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.468,
            0.855,
            0.529
          ],
          "content": "Section 7.3 discusses the Section Formula for the coordinates $(x,y)$ of a point P which divides internally the line segment joining the points $\\mathrm { A } ( \\boldsymbol { x } _ { 1 } , \\boldsymbol { y } _ { 1 } )$ and $\\mathrm{B}(x_{_2},y_{_2})$ in the ratio $m_{_1} : m_{_2}$ as follows :"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.295,
            0.544,
            0.671,
            0.588
          ],
          "content": "x = \\frac{m_{1}x_{2} + m_{2}x_{1}}{m_{1} + m_{2}} , \\quad y = \\frac{m_{1}y_{2} + m_{2}y_{1}}{m_{1} + m_{2}}",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.14,
            0.601,
            0.497,
            0.621
          ],
          "content": "Note that, here, $\\mathrm{PA} \\bar{\\bar{}} : \\mathrm{PB} = m_1 : m_2$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.628,
            0.855,
            0.707
          ],
          "content": "However, if P does not lie between A and B but lies on the line AB, outside the line segment AB, and $\\mathrm{PA}:\\mathrm{PB}=m_{1}:m_{2}$ we say that P divides externally the line segment joining the points A and B. You will study Section Formula for such case in higher classes."
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.967,
            0.563,
            0.982
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        }
      ]
    }
